//! lotus/stereo_width.rye — ALES211: STEREO WIDTH — the image narrowed or widened, by scaling the SIDE and recombining, the first rung to spend
//! ALES210's mid/side primitive. A keeper mixing a stereo master reaches for one knob more than almost any other in the field: WIDTH — pull the
//! image in toward mono to sit a part back in the mix, or push it out past the speakers for air. Width is exactly "scale the side, recombine": the
//! MID (the shared centre) is the mono core, the SIDE (the half-difference) is the stereo spread, so scaling the side alone widens or narrows the
//! image without touching what both channels share.
//!
//! The shape, composing two proven stages with NO new audio arithmetic:
//!   1. encode the master to MID/SIDE in place (ALES210's `mid_side.encode`)
//!   2. scale the SIDE channel by width/den (ALES2's proven `timeline.gain`, which saturates once)
//!   3. decode MID/SIDE back to LEFT/RIGHT in place (ALES210's `mid_side.decode`)
//! Every sample it writes is written by a stage already proven byte for byte; width invents no arithmetic of its own.
//!
//! The three edges name the knob. `width == den` (unity) is the mid/side round-trip — the near-identity, exact on a same-parity master. `width == 0`
//! is the MONO COLLAPSE: the side is zeroed, so both channels become the shared mid, the image pulled fully to centre. `width > den` WIDENS: the side
//! grows, so the channel difference grows, the image pushed outward (saturating at the rail rather than wrapping). A CENTRED master (left == right)
//! has a zero side, so width leaves it untouched at any setting — a mono signal has no width to turn.
//!
//! The width is validated BEFORE any write (a zero or over-bound denominator, or an over-bound numerator, refuses `BadWidth`), and the encode runs
//! only after that check, so a refusal leaves the master untouched and balanced. Once the width is legal the gain over the full valid span and the
//! decode cannot fault — the op refuses whole up front or completes whole.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES211.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-121345_fill-ales211-lotus-stereo-width.md
//! Stands on: lotus/mid_side.rye (ALES210) — the reversible mid/side change of basis, encode and decode, spent whole; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) —
//! the Clip and its proven `gain` (the side scaled by a fraction, saturated once); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip and its balance invariant.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. Width is a pure
//! composition of proven stages over existing samples; it fabricates none, changes no length, and reads no byte past either channel's len. It
//! inherits ALES210's honest ≤ 1 LSB round-trip cost at unity. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_width.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const mid_side = @import("mid_side.rye");

pub const stereo_width_version = "20260815.121345";

/// The widest and narrowest fraction this rung proves. A width is `num/den`: 0 is mono (side zeroed), `den` is unity, past `den` widens. The
/// denominator is bounded like ALES10's pan denominator so a width is a real fraction, and the numerator is bounded so no width can ask for an
/// absurd amplification (the gain saturates anyway; this names the intent). Generous rather than tight — a handful of times unity already pushes an
/// image past the speakers.
pub const max_width_den: u32 = 4096;
pub const max_width_num: u32 = 32768;

/// Width's own fault: a zero or over-bound denominator, or an over-bound numerator, each refused by name BEFORE any write. The gain and decode that
/// follow a legal width cannot fault (the span is the full valid clip), so this is the only error width raises.
pub const StereoWidthError = error{BadWidth};

/// Narrow or widen `sc` by scaling the SIDE by `width_num/width_den` and recombining. `width_den` unity is the mid/side round-trip (the near-identity,
/// exact on a same-parity master); `width_num == 0` collapses to mono (both channels the shared mid); `width_num > width_den` widens (the channel
/// difference grows, saturating at the rail). Refuses `BadWidth` on a zero or over-bound denominator or an over-bound numerator before any write, so
/// a bad width leaves the master untouched and balanced. Values only — never a length.
pub fn stereo_width(sc: *pan.StereoClip, width_num: u32, width_den: u32) StereoWidthError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the width is a real, bounded fraction — a zero denominator is no fraction at all, an over-bound term would ask for an absurd scale.
    // Refused before any write so a bad width never half-transforms the master.
    if (width_den == 0 or width_den > max_width_den or width_num > max_width_num) return error.BadWidth;

    const n: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // 1. Fold LEFT/RIGHT into MID/SIDE in place — ALES210, total, no fault.
    mid_side.encode(sc);
    // 2. Scale the SIDE channel (the right Clip now holds the side) by width_num/width_den — ALES2's proven gain, saturating once. The span is the
    //    full valid clip and the denominator is proven non-zero above, so this cannot raise BadGain or BadRange; the catch restores the master and
    //    re-raises as our own fault, yet the invariant is that it never fires.
    timeline.gain(&sc.right, 0, n, @intCast(width_num), width_den) catch {
        // invariant: unreachable — the width is prechecked legal and the span is the whole clip; restore the master if the impossible happens.
        mid_side.decode(sc);
        return error.BadWidth;
    };
    // 3. Fold MID/SIDE back into LEFT/RIGHT in place — ALES210, total, no fault.
    mid_side.decode(sc);

    // invariant: width writes values only — both channels keep their length, so the master leaves balanced.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len and sc.left.len == n);
}

// --- selftest: THE UNITY EDGE — width == den is the mid/side round-trip, exact on a same-parity master (byte for byte the original); THE MONO
//     COLLAPSE — width == 0 makes both channels the shared mid = (l+r)/2, the image fully centred; THE WIDEN LAW — width == 2·den doubles the side,
//     so the per-sample channel difference |L−R| grows (a wider image), the result differing from the original; THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW — a
//     centred master (l == r) is untouched at any width (a mono signal has no width); THE BALANCE / FAULT LAW — both channels stay balanced, and a
//     zero or over-bound width refuses BadWidth with the master untouched ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // A same-parity master (every pair l+r even) so the mid/side round-trip is exact — the unity and widen laws read against the true original.
    const l0 = [_]i16{ 1000, -2000, 3000, 500 };
    const r0 = [_]i16{ 200, 400, -600, 500 };

    // 1. THE UNITY EDGE — width == den scales the side by one, so stereo_width is exactly decode(encode): on a same-parity master, the original byte
    //    for byte. The knob at rest moves nothing.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_width(&sc, 2, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0)) return error.UnityLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.UnityRightWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE MONO COLLAPSE — width == 0 zeroes the side, so decode gives both channels the shared mid = (l+r)/2: the image is pulled fully to centre,
    //    left == right everywhere. mid: (1000,200)→600, (-2000,400)→-800, (3000,-600)→1200, (500,500)→500.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_width(&sc, 0, 2);
        const mid = [_]i16{ 600, -800, 1200, 500 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &mid)) return error.CollapseLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &mid)) return error.CollapseRightWrong;
        // the defining property: both channels identical — the image is centred, no width left.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.CollapseNotCentred;
    }

    // 3. THE WIDEN LAW — width == 2·den doubles the side, so decode pushes the channels apart: the per-sample difference |L−R| grows against the
    //    original, the image widened. side doubles: 400→800, -1200→-2400, 1800→3600, 0→0; decode l=mid+side, r=mid-side.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_width(&sc, 4, 2); // 2× width
        //  l = mid+2·side: 600+800=1400   -800-2400=-3200   1200+3600=4800   500
        //  r = mid-2·side:  600-800=-200   -800+2400=1600    1200-3600=-2400  500
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1400, -3200, 4800, 500 })) return error.WidenLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -200, 1600, -2400, 500 })) return error.WidenRightWrong;
        // the channel difference has genuinely grown where the master was not centred (the last sample, centred, stays zero-difference).
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < l0.len) : (i += 1) {
            const orig_diff: i32 = @as(i32, l0[i]) - @as(i32, r0[i]);
            const wide_diff: i32 = @as(i32, sc.left.samples()[i]) - @as(i32, sc.right.samples()[i]);
            const oa: i32 = if (orig_diff < 0) -orig_diff else orig_diff;
            const wa: i32 = if (wide_diff < 0) -wide_diff else wide_diff;
            if (wa < oa) return error.WidenDidNotWiden;
        }
    }

    // 4. THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW — a centred master (left == right) has a zero side, so scaling the side by ANY width leaves it untouched: a mono
    //    signal has no width to turn. Proven at the mono collapse and at 2× width, both returning the centred original exactly (l+r = 2l is even).
    {
        const centre = [_]i16{ 5000, -5000, 3000, -2000 };
        var narrow = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&narrow, &centre, &centre);
        try stereo_width(&narrow, 0, 2);
        if (!eq(narrow.left.samples(), &centre) or !eq(narrow.right.samples(), &centre)) return error.CentredNarrowMoved;

        var wide = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&wide, &centre, &centre);
        try stereo_width(&wide, 4, 2);
        if (!eq(wide.left.samples(), &centre) or !eq(wide.right.samples(), &centre)) return error.CentredWideMoved;
    }

    // 5a. THE BALANCE LAW — width holds left.len == right.len and each channel's length, at an even and an odd length; the image stays aligned.
    {
        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ 40, 60, -80 });
        try stereo_width(&odd, 3, 2);
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len or odd.left.len != 3) return error.OddUnbalanced;

        var empty = pan.StereoClip{};
        try stereo_width(&empty, 0, 2);
        if (empty.left.len != 0 or empty.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyChanged;
    }

    // 5b. THE FAULT LAW — a zero denominator, an over-bound denominator, and an over-bound numerator each refuse BadWidth BEFORE any write, so the
    //     master is left untouched and balanced (the width is checked before encode ever runs).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        if (stereo_width(&sc, 2, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadWidth) return error.WrongZeroDen;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.ZeroDenMutated;
        if (stereo_width(&sc, 2, max_width_den + 1)) |_| return error.OverDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadWidth) return error.WrongOverDen;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.OverDenMutated;
        if (stereo_width(&sc, max_width_num + 1, 2)) |_| return error.OverNumSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadWidth) return error.WrongOverNum;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.OverNumMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-width: STEREO WIDTH — the image narrowed or widened by scaling the SIDE and recombining, the first rung to spend " ++
        "ALES210's mid/side primitive. Width is exactly 'scale the side, recombine': the MID is the mono core both channels share, the SIDE the " ++
        "stereo spread, so scaling the side alone widens or narrows the image without touching the centre. The shape composes two proven stages " ++
        "with NO new audio arithmetic: encode to mid/side (ALES210), scale the side by width/den (ALES2's proven gain, saturating once), decode " ++
        "back (ALES210). The three edges name the knob. THE UNITY EDGE: width == den is the mid/side round-trip, exact on a same-parity master (the " ++
        "original byte for byte). THE MONO COLLAPSE: width == 0 zeroes the side, so both channels become the shared mid = (l+r)/2, the image pulled " ++
        "fully to centre. THE WIDEN LAW: width == 2·den doubles the side, so the per-sample channel difference |L−R| grows, the image pushed " ++
        "outward (saturating at the rail, never wrapping). THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW: a centred master (l == r) has a zero side, so width leaves it " ++
        "untouched at any setting — a mono signal has no width to turn. THE BALANCE / FAULT LAW: both channels stay balanced, and a zero or " ++
        "over-bound width refuses BadWidth before any write, the master untouched. Purely local — a pure composition of proven stages over existing " ++
        "i16 samples, inheriting ALES210's honest ≤ 1 LSB round-trip cost at unity; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real " ++
        "speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
